For $X\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)$:


1. derive $G_p(p)=1/[p(1-p)]$;

2. switch to the logit and obtain $G_z(z)=p(1-p)$;

3. show that $u(p)=2\arcsin\sqrt p$ transforms the line element into $ds^2=du^2$;

4. compute the Fisher–Rao distance between $p_1=0.1$ and $p_2=0.9$.

For $Y\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(q)$ and a model $p=\sigma(z)$:


1. derive the model Fisher $p(1-p)$;

2. compute $\mathbb E_{Y\sim q}[(Y-p)^2]$;

3. determine when the two quantities coincide;

4. explain why coincidence at an optimum does not make empirical Fisher a universal curvature matrix.

Run

```text
M15_information_geometry_experiments.py
```

The script


1. compares exact Bernoulli KL with its quadratic Fisher approximation;

2. demonstrates coordinate dependence of ordinary gradient and consistency of the natural-gradient flow;

3. compares model Fisher with the data-gradient second moment;

4. constructs the PPO counterexample;

5. compares optimal and independent couplings for a Gaussian path.
